(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-pulmonary-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Physiology — Quiz 1 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During heavy exercise, forced expiration becomes active and is driven largely by...","choices":["the release of surfactant that forces air out under these conditions","contraction of the diaphragm to push air outward","contraction of the abdominal and internal intercostal muscles","relaxation of the diaphragm alone, as in quiet breathing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Whereas quiet expiration is passive, forced expiration recruits the abdominal muscles (which push the diaphragm upward) and the internal intercostals (which pull the ribs down), actively decreasing thoracic volume to expel air rapidly during exertion. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-14"},{"q":"External respiration and internal respiration refer, respectively, to gas exchange...","choices":["only within the airways, and only within the bloodstream","between alveoli and blood, and between blood and the body's tissues","between the nose and the trachea, and within the alveoli","between blood and tissues, and between alveoli and blood as a general rule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. External respiration is the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide between the alveoli and the pulmonary blood, while internal respiration is the exchange between the systemic blood and the metabolizing tissues; both rely on diffusion down partial-pressure gradients. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 3-6, 71-74"},{"q":"The principal muscle responsible for quiet inspiration is the...","choices":["diaphragm, whose contraction increases the vertical dimension of the thorax","sternocleidomastoid muscle, which is said to be the primary driver at rest","internal intercostal group, which actively pulls the ribs downward","abdominal wall, which pushes the diaphragm upward to draw in air"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. During quiet breathing the diaphragm does most of the work; its contraction flattens the dome and lengthens the thoracic cavity, lowering intrapleural and alveolar pressure so air flows in. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-14"},{"q":"If air enters the pleural space and abolishes the negative pressure, the lung tends to...","choices":["recoil and collapse because nothing opposes its elastic pull","remain unchanged since pleural pressure is irrelevant","stiffen noticeably and become nearly impossible to compress","expand further because recoil is now unopposed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lung's inherent elastic recoil pulls it inward; the negative pleural pressure normally counteracts this. If air enters the pleural space (pneumothorax) and abolishes that negative pressure, the recoil is unopposed and the lung collapses. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 19, 50"},{"q":"Surface tension at the air-water interface lining the alveoli tends to...","choices":["increase lung compliance by making expansion easier","draw the alveolar walls together and favor collapse","have no measurable effect on alveolar stability at any size","hold the alveoli open by pushing their walls apart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: surface tension pulls the liquid-lined walls inward, favoring alveolar collapse unless surfactant opposes it. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 19-20"},{"q":"By the law of Laplace, without surfactant a small alveolus would tend to...","choices":["collapse and empty into a connected larger alveolus","become completely rigid and unable to change size","expand indefinitely at the expense of larger alveoli","generate less collapsing pressure than a large alveolus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Laplace's law says that for a given surface tension, smaller alveoli generate higher collapsing pressure; without surfactant, a small alveolus would empty into a larger connected one. Surfactant lowers tension more in small alveoli, stabilizing them. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-24"},{"q":"Pulmonary surfactant is produced by...","choices":["type I alveolar cells, forming the main gas-exchange surface","goblet cells lining the large conducting airways under these conditions","type II alveolar cells and lowers surface tension in the alveoli","alveolar macrophages that also clear inhaled debris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Type II alveolar (pneumocyte) cells secrete surfactant, a phospholipid-rich material that lowers alveolar surface tension, stabilizes alveoli of different sizes, and reduces the work of breathing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Surfactant is chiefly composed of...","choices":["phospholipid, which interposes between water molecules to reduce surface tension","cartilage, which stiffens the alveolar wall for support","hemoglobin, which binds oxygen within the alveolus under most conditions in the body","mucus, which traps inhaled particles in the alveoli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Surfactant is largely phospholipid (notably dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine); its molecules insert among the water molecules lining the alveoli, weakening their cohesive attraction and thereby lowering surface tension. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Alveolar ventilation is always less than total minute ventilation because...","choices":["the alveoli are able to exchange gas only during the expiratory phase","part of each breath fills conducting airways that do no gas exchange","oxygen is consumed before air can reach the alveoli","dead-space air is the only air that reaches the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each tidal breath must first fill the anatomic dead space (~150 mL of conducting airway), and that air never reaches the alveoli for exchange, so the ventilation available for gas exchange (alveolar ventilation) is less than the total minute ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"Residual volume cannot be measured by ordinary spirometry because it is the air that...","choices":["is exhaled with every ordinary quiet breath during this process","moves through the conducting airways each minute","can be forcibly inhaled beyond a normal breath","remains in the lungs and can never be voluntarily exhaled"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Residual volume is the air left in the lungs even after a maximal expiration; since it can never be exhaled, spirometry (which measures moved air) cannot capture it, and indirect methods such as gas dilution are required. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 30-31"},{"q":"Alveolar ventilation is a better measure of effective breathing than minute ventilation because it...","choices":["accounts for dead space, reflecting only air that reaches the alveoli","ignores the tidal volume entirely in its calculation as a general rule","measures only the residual volume left after expiration","counts all air moved, including that filling the airways"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alveolar ventilation subtracts the dead-space volume (air in conducting airways that never reaches alveoli) from each breath, so it reflects only the air available for gas exchange; this is why slow, deep breathing can be more effective than rapid, shallow breathing at the same minute ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"Oxygen moves from alveolar air into pulmonary capillary blood because...","choices":["the blood actively pumps oxygen out of the alveolus as it is usually described here","carbon dioxide drags oxygen along with it into the blood","alveolar oxygen partial pressure is higher than that in the arriving blood","alveolar oxygen pressure is lower than that in the blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gas exchange is driven by partial-pressure gradients: alveolar PO2 (~100 mmHg) exceeds the PO2 of the venous blood entering the pulmonary capillaries (~40 mmHg), so oxygen diffuses down its gradient into the blood until equilibration. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 61-66"},{"q":"In the upright lung, ventilation and perfusion are not perfectly matched; at the apex compared with the base there is...","choices":["exactly equal ventilation and perfusion throughout as a general rule","no ventilation and no perfusion reaching that region","relatively more perfusion than ventilation than the base","relatively more ventilation than perfusion (a higher V/Q ratio)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gravity makes both ventilation and perfusion greater at the base, but perfusion increases more steeply, so the apex has a relatively higher ventilation-to-perfusion ratio, while the base has a relatively lower one. This regional variation is normal in the upright lung. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 64-66"},{"q":"The respiratory membrane is well suited to gas exchange because it is...","choices":["covered by thick mucus that traps the diffusing gases","extremely thin, so gases have only a very short distance to diffuse","quite thick, which slows diffusion but filters the air","lined with cartilage that supports rapid exchange in the body's tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The respiratory membrane is only about 0.2 to 0.6 micrometers thick, giving oxygen and carbon dioxide a very short diffusion path; combined with its enormous surface area, this thinness makes gas exchange rapid and efficient. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 57-60"},{"q":"The anatomic dead space refers to...","choices":["the alveoli that receive air but no blood flow","the total volume of air moved in one minute","the residual air that can never be exhaled during this process","the volume of conducting airways where no gas exchange occurs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anatomic dead space is the air filling the conducting zone — nose, trachea, and bronchi — which has no alveoli and therefore participates in no gas exchange; only the air reaching the alveoli contributes to gas exchange. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"The peripheral chemoreceptors' oxygen response becomes an important drive to breathe mainly when...","choices":["blood pressure rises at the carotid sinus","carbon dioxide is the only gas that has changed","arterial oxygen falls to notably low levels","arterial oxygen is completely normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The carotid and aortic bodies respond to low arterial PO2, but this hypoxic drive becomes significant only when PO2 falls well below normal (roughly under 60 mmHg); under normal conditions, CO2 is the dominant stimulus and oxygen contributes little. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 95, 99"},{"q":"The carotid and aortic bodies are especially important for detecting...","choices":["a rise in brain interstitial carbon dioxide concentration","a fall in the arterial partial pressure of oxygen","the pH of the cerebrospinal fluid around the medulla","stretch of the airway smooth muscle during inspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies are the main sensors of low arterial oxygen. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 95, 97"},{"q":"The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex helps protect the lungs by...","choices":["inhibiting further inspiration when stretch receptors sense large inflation","forcing a deeper breath whenever the lungs are stretched","contracting the diaphragm independently of the brainstem","sensing arterial oxygen and driving ventilation during this particular process"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stretch receptors in the airway walls, when strongly stimulated by large lung inflation, send signals that inhibit the inspiratory center; this reflex helps prevent overinflation of the lungs, mainly during deep breathing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 89-90"},{"q":"The Bohr effect describes how increased CO2 and H+ in active tissue...","choices":["convert oxygen into bicarbonate for transport in the body under most conditions","prevent hemoglobin from binding any oxygen in the lungs","shift the curve leftward, making hemoglobin hold oxygen tightly","shift the curve rightward, helping hemoglobin release more oxygen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Bohr effect is the rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve caused by higher CO2, higher H+ (lower pH), and higher temperature in metabolically active tissue; the reduced affinity promotes oxygen unloading exactly where it is needed. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 81"},{"q":"The reason hemoglobin is essential for oxygen transport is that, without it,...","choices":["carbon dioxide would be unable to leave the tissues","the small amount of oxygen dissolved in plasma could not meet tissue demands","oxygen could not diffuse across the respiratory membrane","the lungs would be unable to ventilate at all during this process in the tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is poorly soluble, so plasma alone carries only a tiny fraction of the body's needs; hemoglobin increases the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity many-fold by binding oxygen, which is why anemia (low hemoglobin) so seriously reduces oxygen delivery. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 76-77"},{"q":"The P50 of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve is the partial pressure at which hemoglobin is...","choices":["binding carbon dioxide rather than oxygen as it typically occurs here","fully saturated with the maximum amount of oxygen","50% saturated, serving as a measure of its affinity for oxygen","completely stripped of all of its bound oxygen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. P50 is the PO2 at which hemoglobin is half saturated; a higher P50 means lower affinity (the curve is shifted right, favoring unloading), and a lower P50 means higher affinity (left shift), so P50 is a convenient index of oxygen affinity. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 78-81"},{"q":"During exercise, working muscle extracts more oxygen from each unit of blood because...","choices":["low tissue PO2 and a rightward curve shift favor greater unloading","hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen rises in active tissue","oxygen is actively pumped back out into the blood from the muscle","blood flow to the muscle stops during contraction under these conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exercising muscle has a very low PO2 and generates heat, CO2, and acid that shift the oxygen-hemoglobin curve rightward; together these steepen the gradient and lower hemoglobin's affinity, so much more oxygen is unloaded per unit of blood than at rest. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 80-81"},{"q":"A rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve in exercising muscle...","choices":["occurs mainly in the lungs, where it instead speeds pulmonary oxygen loading","prevents any oxygen from being released to the surrounding tissues","increases hemoglobin's oxygen affinity so it holds oxygen more tightly","promotes oxygen unloading where tissue CO2, acid, and temperature are high"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a rightward shift lowers affinity, enhancing oxygen release in active tissues with high CO2, low pH, and heat. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 81"},{"q":"Factors such as increased temperature, CO2, and acidity in metabolically active tissue tend to...","choices":["increase hemoglobin's affinity so it retains its oxygen","block hemoglobin from picking up any oxygen at all in the lungs","reduce oxygen unloading, sparing oxygen for later use","promote oxygen unloading from hemoglobin where it is most needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These conditions mark actively metabolizing tissue and shift the oxygen-hemoglobin curve rightward, lowering affinity so hemoglobin releases more oxygen precisely at the tissues that are working hardest. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 80-81"},{"q":"Carbon dioxide is transported in blood in three forms; the largest fraction travels as...","choices":["dissolved CO2 gas in the plasma","carbaminohemoglobin bound to globin","bicarbonate ions dissolved in the plasma","carbonic acid contained within the red blood cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The majority of CO2 (about 70%) is carried as bicarbonate formed after carbonic anhydrase hydrates CO2 in red cells; smaller fractions travel bound to hemoglobin (carbamino, ~23%) or dissolved as free CO2 (~7%). — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-86"},{"q":"Carbonic anhydrase speeds CO2 transport because it...","choices":["rapidly converts CO2 and water into carbonic acid inside red cells","binds the CO2 molecule directly onto the heme iron of hemoglobin","pumps bicarbonate out of the plasma into the lungs","destroys excess CO2 before it can be transported during this particular process"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase in red cells greatly accelerates the hydration of CO2 to carbonic acid, which then dissociates to bicarbonate and H+; this lets the blood carry the large majority of its CO2 as bicarbonate and is central to CO2 transport and acid-base handling. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-87"},{"q":"A portion of CO2 is carried bound to hemoglobin as carbaminohemoglobin; this binding occurs at...","choices":["the plasma proteins outside the red cell","the globin portion, not the heme iron where oxygen binds","the same heme iron site used by oxygen","the carbonic anhydrase enzyme molecule itself within the cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbamino CO2 attaches to amino groups on the globin chains of hemoglobin, a different site from the heme iron that binds oxygen; this is why hemoglobin can carry oxygen and some CO2 simultaneously. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-86"},{"q":"The bicarbonate buffer system links respiration to acid-base balance because...","choices":["the buffer works only in the kidney and not in the blood under most conditions in the body","hemoglobin converts bicarbonate into oxygen for transport","bicarbonate is exhaled directly by the lungs to remove acid","CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates to H+ and bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. CO2 + H2O reversibly forms carbonic acid, which dissociates into H+ and bicarbonate. Because the lungs control CO2, changing ventilation shifts this equilibrium and thus blood pH — the mechanistic link between breathing and acid-base status. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"Acidosis and alkalosis are defined by blood pH such that acidosis is a...","choices":["shift toward a higher-than-normal pH (fewer hydrogen ions)","shift toward a lower-than-normal pH (more hydrogen ions)","state in which blood pH cannot change at all","condition affecting only oxygen levels, not pH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acidosis refers to a process lowering blood pH below the normal range (an increase in hydrogen ion concentration), while alkalosis refers to a process raising pH above normal; each can arise from respiratory (CO2) or metabolic causes. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"A disturbance is called a respiratory (rather than metabolic) acid-base problem when it originates from...","choices":["an accumulation of lactic acid during exercise","the loss of stomach acid through vomiting","a change in CO2 caused by altered ventilation","a change in bicarbonate produced by the kidneys"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory acid-base disturbances arise from changes in CO2 due to hypo- or hyperventilation (CO2 retention lowers pH; excess CO2 removal raises it), whereas metabolic disturbances arise from changes in bicarbonate or fixed acids independent of CO2. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"}]};
